𝐊𝐮𝐛𝐞𝐫𝐧𝐞𝐭𝐞𝐬 𝐀𝐫𝐜𝐡𝐢𝐭𝐞𝐜𝐭𝐮𝐫𝐞: 𝐀𝐭 𝐚 𝐆𝐥𝐚𝐧𝐜𝐞 ☸️ :

Understanding Kubernetes can feel overwhelming, but here’s a quick rundown of what each part does.

𝑪𝒐𝒏𝒕𝒓𝒐𝒍 𝑷𝒍𝒂𝒏𝒆 :
𝐀𝐏𝐈 𝐒𝐞𝐫𝐯𝐞𝐫: The “front desk” – receives all commands (kubectl/ API/ dashboard).

𝐞𝐭𝐜𝐝: The “memory” – stores all cluster data/configs.

𝐒𝐜𝐡𝐞𝐝𝐮𝐥𝐞𝐫: The “planner” – decides which node runs each new Pod.

𝐂𝐨𝐧𝐭𝐫𝐨𝐥𝐥𝐞𝐫 𝐌𝐚𝐧𝐚𝐠𝐞𝐫: The “supervisor” – keeps the cluster in the desired state.

𝐂𝐥𝐨𝐮𝐝 𝐂𝐨𝐧𝐭𝐫𝐨𝐥𝐥𝐞𝐫 𝐌𝐚𝐧𝐚𝐠𝐞𝐫: The “cloud connector” – integrates with your cloud provider.

𝑫𝒂𝒕𝒂 𝑷𝒍𝒂𝒏𝒆 (𝑾𝒐𝒓𝒌𝒆𝒓 𝑵𝒐𝒅𝒆):
𝐍𝐚𝐦𝐞𝐬𝐩𝐚𝐜𝐞: Organizes cluster resources for multi-tenancy and access control.

𝐊𝐮𝐛𝐞𝐥𝐞𝐭: The “executor” – ensures containers run as instructed.

𝐂𝐑𝐈 (𝐂𝐨𝐧𝐭𝐚𝐢𝐧𝐞𝐫 𝐑𝐮𝐧𝐭𝐢𝐦𝐞 𝐈𝐧𝐭𝐞𝐫𝐟𝐚𝐜𝐞): The “engine connector” – talks to Docker/containerd to launch containers.

𝐊𝐮𝐛𝐞-𝐩𝐫𝐨𝐱𝐲: The “networker” – routes traffic to the right Pods.

𝑪𝒐𝒏𝒕𝒂𝒊𝒏𝒆𝒓 𝑹𝒖𝒏𝒕𝒊𝒎𝒆:
𝐏𝐨𝐝𝐬: The smallest deployable units – can hold one or more containers.

𝑲𝒖𝒃𝒆𝒓𝒏𝒆𝒕𝒆𝒔 𝑶𝒃𝒋𝒆𝒄𝒕𝒔:
𝐃𝐞𝐩𝐥𝐨𝐲𝐦𝐞𝐧𝐭 (𝐝𝐞𝐩𝐥𝐨𝐲): Deployments for rolling updates & scaling.

𝐒𝐞𝐫𝐯𝐢𝐜𝐞 (𝐬𝐯𝐜): Services for exposing Pods.

𝐈𝐧𝐠𝐫𝐞𝐬𝐬 (𝐢𝐧𝐠): Ingress for external access (HTTP/HTTPS).

𝐃𝐚𝐞𝐦𝐨𝐧𝐒𝐞𝐭 (𝐝𝐬): DaemonSets for running Pods on every node.

𝐂𝐨𝐧𝐟𝐢𝐠𝐌𝐚𝐩: Stores non-sensitive configuration data separately from Pods.

𝐒𝐞𝐜𝐫𝐞𝐭: Securely stores sensitive data.

𝐑𝐞𝐩𝐥𝐢𝐜𝐚𝐒𝐞𝐭 (𝐫𝐬): ReplicaSets for ensuring the right number of Pods.

𝐉𝐨𝐛: Runs Pods for a specific task and stops.

𝐇𝐨𝐫𝐢𝐳𝐨𝐧𝐭𝐚𝐥 𝐏𝐨𝐝 𝐀𝐮𝐭𝐨𝐬𝐜𝐚𝐥𝐞𝐫 (𝐡𝐩𝐚): Autoscaler for scaling Pods up/down.

𝐒𝐭𝐨𝐫𝐚𝐠𝐞𝐂𝐥𝐚𝐬𝐬 (𝐬𝐜), 𝐏𝐞𝐫𝐬𝐢𝐬𝐭𝐞𝐧𝐭𝐕𝐨𝐥𝐮𝐦𝐞𝐂𝐥𝐚𝐢𝐦 (𝐩𝐯𝐜), 𝐏𝐞𝐫𝐬𝐢𝐬𝐭𝐞𝐧𝐭𝐕𝐨𝐥𝐮𝐦𝐞 (𝐩𝐯): Storage management.

𝐒𝐭𝐚𝐭𝐞𝐟𝐮𝐥𝐒𝐞𝐭 (𝐬𝐭𝐬): StatefulSets for apps needing stable identities.

